WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.450
As fun as it is to watch a great movie, there’s
no guarantee that you’ll walk out of the

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:05.740
theater feeling good yourself.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:10.760
Here are a few fantastic flicks where the
last few minutes are so monumentally depressing

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.980
that they’re almost guaranteed to wring
a few tears from the audience.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.850
If you only saw the last three minutes of
End of Watch, you might think this cop drama

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.520
was ending on a happy note.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:25.020
The final scene features two buddies having
a laugh over a crazy story on what looks like

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.040
a pretty good day.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.600
Unfortunately, context is everything.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.200
In actuality, the camaraderie between Officers
Brian Taylor and Mike Zavala is an incredibly

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.770
bittersweet flashback scene, one that jumps
back to a moment before Zavala was gunned

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:39.860
down by gangsters.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.480
Taylor and Zavala are super cops.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.750
In fact, they're such good cops that they
try to take down the Sinaloa Cartel… only

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.360
that doesn't pan out too well.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.560
After disrupting the cartel's business, the
officers are ambushed by heavily armed thugs,

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.960
and despite their bravery, Taylor is gravely
injured, and Zavala is murdered.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.340
After learning about his partner's death,
Taylor is absolutely shattered.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.280
Zavala was his best friend in the world, and
at the funeral, Taylor is so grief-stricken

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.600
that he can only muster four powerful words
that make all those happy moments heartbreaking:

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:12.960
“He was my brother.”

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.780
Thanos first appeared in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe all the way back in 2012, but when

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.220
he finally arrived on Earth with the Infinity
Gauntlet, Earth's mightiest heroes were completely

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.220
overmatched.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.120
With a single snap of his fingers, Thanos
wiped out half the population of the universe,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.300
including the majority of our beloved heroes.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.620
Black Panther, Doctor Strange and even poor
Teen Groot went up in smoke.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.840
The most painful death to watch, though, was
Peter Parker's.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.260
“Mister Stark, I don’t feel so good.”

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.340
Even though he just fought the toughest villain
in the MCU, Peter's still just a kid, afraid

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.300
of fading away.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:56.220
Avengers: Endgame will undoubtedly undo a
lot of the damage, but when a horrified Captain

00:01:56.229 --> 00:02:00.900
America - the team's strongest and most optimistic
member - is this wrecked, you know things

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:01.660
have gone bad.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.380
Infinity War might have the darkest ending
of any major blockbuster ever.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.120
There's no moral victory here; there's no
feel-good speech.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:15.219
For the first time ever, the Avengers have
truly lost, and it all ends with Thanos admiring

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.840
his handiwork, smiling as the sun rises on
a "grateful universe."

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.060
Despite their god-like superpowers, the Avengers
are a sad bunch of people, and the most tragic

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.960
might just be Steve Rogers, a man out of time.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:33.359
In Captain America: The First Avenger, the
star-spangled superhero decides to sacrifice

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.569
his life, crashing a Hydra plane into the
Arctic ice.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:41.720
In those final moments before taking the plunge,
Cap promises to take his new love Peggy Carter

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.240
dancing as soon as he returns.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.240
Obviously, neither expect Steve to make it
back home, but, well, you’ve seen the movies.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.060
“I understood that reference.”

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:56.760
When Cap finally returns, he learns that he’s
been asleep for nearly seven decades.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.379
It's a gut punch of a moment, and the look
of shock and sadness on Cap's face is heartbreaking.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.040
Yeah, he survived the suicide mission, but
the world he knew is gone, and he’s been

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.480
separated from the woman he loves for most
of a century.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:14.060
When Fury finally asks if Cap is going to
be okay, all his pain is summed up in one

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:14.560
sentence:

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:15.560
"Yeah.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:21.540
Yeah, I just — I had a date."

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:27.200
War movies aren't really known for their upbeat
endings, but when it comes to pure tearjerking

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.120
power, nothing goes after your heartstrings
like 1989’s Glory.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:36.040
This Civil War flick tells the story of the
54th Massachusetts Infantry, which was the

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.860
first African-American regiment to fight for
the Union army.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.019
The regiment was led by Colonel Robert Shaw,
played in the film by Matthew Broderick, and

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:49.069
after overcoming all the racism you’d expect
from the 1860s, Shaw and the 54th wind up

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:51.480
leading the charge in the film's climactic
battle.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.800
Unfortunately, that battle doesn’t go their
way.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:59.780
After being surrounded by Confederate soldiers,
Shaw charges the enemy, hoping to inspire

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:01.520
his men, and he's quickly gunned down.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.240
Enraged, Private Silas Trip, a role that earned
Denzel Washington an Academy Award, picks

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:11.440
up the flag and charges after the colonel,
only to be shot down seconds later.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.340
Their sacrifice inspires the rest of the 54th
to rush out of the ravine and continue their

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:21.720
charge, but despite their bravery, the heroic
soldiers are no match for cannonballs, and

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:25.990
all the characters we've come to love - played
by the likes of Morgan Freeman, Cary Elwes,

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:28.480
and Andre Braugher - are blown to oblivion.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.060
If that wasn't sad enough, the movie ends
with Shaw's and Trip's bodies being buried

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.160
side by side in a mass grave.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.920
Gone Baby Gone isn’t exactly a feel-good
film to begin with, and the ending is appropriately

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:41.680
downbeat.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.860
Based on the novel by Dennis Lehane, this
thriller follows two Boston detectives - Patrick

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.000
Kenzie and Angie Gennaro - who are hired to
find a little girl named Amanda.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:55.540
At first, they believe the kid was murdered
by a local drug dealer, but as they dig deeper

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:58.460
into the case, they discover a surprising
conspiracy.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:03.650
As it turns out, Amanda's concerned uncle
kidnapped her from her abusive, addict mother

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:08.420
with the help of several high-ranking cops,
but Patrick isn't a big believer in moral

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:12.759
ambiguity, and brings brings the conspiracy
down and has the little girl sent back home.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.480
“You may not regret it when you get home.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.620
You may not regret it for a year.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.880
But when get to where I am, I promise you,
you will.”

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.780
Sure enough, it doesn’t take long for regret
to set in when Patrick goes to visit Amanda.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.930
He sees that her mom is just as awful as ever
and realizes he might've damned Amanda to

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:32.340
a life of poverty, neglect, and mistreatment.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:37.160
In the final few moments, he volunteers to
babysit Amanda and sits beside her, watching

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.200
the traumatized, lonely girl as she stares
at the television.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:45.840
It might seem weird to say this about a movie
where a gorilla fights a helicopter, but Rise

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.920
of the Planet of the Apes has more than its
fair share of emotional moments.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:55.340
Nothing can top the finale, when Caesar, ably
played by mo-cap master Andy Serkis, says

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:57.680
goodbye to his human friend, Will Rodman.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.000
Their relationship is the emotional crux of
the story.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:06.020
Caesar has known Will his entire life, and
the biologist is basically his adoptive dad.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:10.020
In fact, Will is the guy who gave Caesar is
above-average intelligence.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.440
But after Will is forced to put Caesar in
a primate sanctuary, the chimpanzee decides

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:15.940
it's time for a revolution.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:21.440
After sparking a full-blown primate rebellion
and a battle on the Golden Gate Bridge, Caesar

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:23.820
and his army disappear into a redwood forest.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.700
But before vanishing into the trees, Caesar
is confronted by Will, who begs the chimp

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:29.840
to come back home.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:33.340
That's when Caesar pulls his foster father
close and whispers into his ear.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.740
"Caesar is home."

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:42.300
With those three powerful words, Caesar and
Will realize nothing will ever be the same.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:47.419
Tom Hanks movies generally leave audiences
feeling pretty good, but Road to Perdition

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.740
is a bloody exception to that cheerful rule.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:55.380
This gangster flick finds Hanks playing Michael
Sullivan Sr., a mob enforcer out for revenge

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:59.560
while trying to make sure his surviving son,
Michael Jr, doesn't wind up dead too.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.760
That's easier said than done when you're being
hunted by a psychopathic hitman.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.080
For a moment, it seems like everything might
be okay.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:11.300
Then, Michael's violent past catches up with
him when he's ambushed by the assassin.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:16.140
After taking two bullets to the back, Michael
lies dying as the psychopathic hitman assembles

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.520
an old-timey camera and begins taking photos
of his bloody victim.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:24.500
Michael Jr. shows up with a gun to interrupt
the hitman’s sick hobby, but unlike his

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.180
dad, the boy isn't a killer.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.379
Fortunately, the distraction gives his father
enough time to gun down the assassin before

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.090
shuffling off this mortal coil.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:37.260
What really seals a deal and cues the sobbing,
though, is Michael Jr.'s final lines, answering

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.480
the question of whether his father was a good
man:

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:41.500
"I always give the same answer.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:47.200
I just tell them he was my father."

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.900
Directed by Martin Scorsese, Shutter Island
is a frightening film noir that finds Leonardo

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:58.159
DiCaprio as Teddy Daniels, a detective who's
investigating a disappearance on an Alcatraz-style

00:07:58.160 --> 00:07:59.159
mental hospital.

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:03.660
But as Teddy and his partner dig deeper into
the case, Teddy makes a horrible discovery:

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.100
he isn't really Teddy.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:10.439
In reality, he's Andrew Laeddis, a World War
II vet who killed his mentally ill wife after

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:11.840
she murdered their kids.

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:16.360
His partner is actually his psychologist,
and the whole investigation is an elaborate

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:19.180
role-playing game meant to bring Andrew back
to reality.

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:22.900
And if the game doesn't work, then poor Andrew
has to get lobotomized.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:28.240
After this shocking twist, it seems that Andrew
reverts back to his Teddy persona, forcing

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.140
his doctors to perform the lobotomy.

00:08:30.629 --> 00:08:34.920
But before he's taken away to his mind-numbing
fate, Teddy looks over at Chuck and asks a

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:36.100
haunting question:

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:38.400
"Which would be worse?

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:44.140
To live as a monster, or to die as a good
man?"

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.620
It’s a simple line, but it conveys the crushing
sadness of Andrew’s story.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:50.120
The experiment worked.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:55.000
Andrew knows exactly who he is and what he’s
done, It’s his guilt that leads him to accept

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:56.010
his grisly fate.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:00.949
Arnold Schwarzenegger movies aren't generally
considered tearjerkers, but the ending of

00:09:00.949 --> 00:09:06.240
Terminator 2: Judgment Day has caused so many
sobs from macho movie fans that it’s basically

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.380
the action movie equivalent of The Notebook.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:10.020
“You think I’m unemotional, don’t you?

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:11.040
I can be emotional!

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.080
Jesus, I cried like a child at the end of
Terminator 2!”

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:19.660
Sent from the future to protect teenage John
Connor, the T-800 starts out this sci-fi flick

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:21.420
as an ice cold killer robot.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:26.180
As he spends time with the future resistance
leader, however, the T-800 becomes the teen's

00:09:26.199 --> 00:09:30.940
best friend and father figure growing increasingly
lovable as he battles with a shapeshifting

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:31.580
android.

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.340
He's picking up slang words, developing human
emotions, and learning you can't just go around

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:37.420
killing people.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.519
He also realizes that if humanity is going
to survive, then all Terminators need to be

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:43.020
destroyed.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:48.060
After defeating the T-1000, Arnold realizes
that if he doesn't destroy the advanced AI

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:52.600
inside his own head, that tech will eventually
give rise to the robot rebellion that will

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:53.440
doom mankind.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:59.030
So despite John's pleas for him to stay, the
T-800 slowly lowers himself into a pit of

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:00.100
molten steel.

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:04.680
He gives a thumbs up before disintegrating
completely, letting John know everything is

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:05.699
going to be okay.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:10.600
But, despite that reassurance, poor John is
in tears, and so is everybody watching the

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.800
saddest ending in Schwarzenegger's career.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:20.360
"I know now why you cry, but it's something
I can never do."

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:26.040
With movies like Requiem for a Dream in his
filmography, it's safe to say that Darren

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:31.000
Aronofsky loves a truly depressing ending,
but of all the films he's ever directed, none

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.320
have caused as much sobbing as The Wrestler.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:38.730
This gritty and grim drama follows the story
of Randy "The Ram" Robinson, a professional

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:41.540
wrestler who's far from his in-ring glory
days.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:46.640
This dude has a lived a hard life and made
some bad decisions, and now he's all alone.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:51.120
His daughter doesn't want anything to do with
him, his romantic life isn't working out,

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.200
and the world outside the ring is nothing
but pain and misery.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:58.880
Lonely and depressed, Randy gets back into
the wrestling game, where at least the fans

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:00.140
respect and adore him.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:03.680
But stepping back into the ring comes at a
high price.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:07.780
Randy has a bad heart and knows that another
match might be his last.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.420
However, when he hears the cheers of the crowd,
the wrestler climbs up on the turnbuckle to

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:16.380
perform his signature move… even as his
heart starts to fail him.

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:21.089
When he leaps down onto his opponent, we know
it's the last time he'll ever do the legendary

00:11:21.089 --> 00:11:25.570
"Ram Jam,” but it's all worth for it Randy,
because he's living and dying for the only

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:30.740
family he's ever known, the fans… who now
have to watch their hero die in the ring in

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:31.300
front of them.

